hello and welcome to med simplified my  name is dr umar malik and in this video  i will tell you about top 10 health  benefits of flax seeds for centuries  flax seeds have been priced for their  health protective properties in fact  going back to the 8th century king  charles the great ordered his subjects  to eat flax seeds to improve their  health  so it's no wonder that flax seeds were  given the name linum ucitasimum  now 13 centuries later some experts say  we have preliminary research to pack up  what charles the great suspected  flax seeds are glossy brown in color and  are shaped like an apple pip which is  about four to seven millimeters long  the earliest evidence of humans using  wild flax was in the form of textiles  spun dyed and knotted wild flax fibers  were found in caves currently situated  in present-day republic of georgia  scientists estimate that these fibers  were dated to more than 10 000 years ago  today flaxseed is found in all kinds of  foods ranging from crackers to waffles  to oatmeal  flax seeds are emerging as a superfood  as more scientific research points to  their health benefits  so here are 10 health benefits of flax  seeds that are backed by science  number one flax seeds are loaded with  nutrients  flax has been grown since the beginning  of civilization and is one of the oldest  crops there are two types of flax seeds  brown and golden which are equally  nutritious  a typical serving size for grounded flax  seeds is 1 tbsp which is about 7 grams  just 1 tablespoon provides a good amount  of protein fiber and omega-3 fatty acids  in addition to being a rich source of  some key vitamins and minerals just to  look at how nutritious these seeds are  one tablespoon of flax seeds contains 37  calories 1.3 grams of protein 2 grams of  carbs and 1.9 grams of fiber  it also contains 3 grams of fat which  includes 1600 milligrams of omega-3  fatty acids  it contains some important vitamins like  vitamin b1 and vitamin b6 and folate it  also contains very important minerals  like calcium iron magnesium phosphorus  and potassium  interestingly flax seeds health benefits  are mainly attributed to the presence of  omega-3 fatty acids lignans and the  fiber present in them  now let's talk about the number second  important health benefit of flax seeds  which is the presence of high amounts of  omega-3 fats in them flax seeds are a  rich source of alpha-linolenic acid ala  which is plant-based omega-3 fatty acid  ala is one of the two essential fatty  acids that you have to obtain from the  food you eat as your body doesn't  produce them  animal studies have shown that the ala  in flax seeds prevented cholesterol from  being deposited in the blood vessels of  the heart reduced inflammation in the  arteries and reduced tumor growth  a costa rican study involving  3638 people found that those who ate  more ala had a lower risk of heart  attack than those who consumed less  amount of ala  also a large review of 27 studies  involving more than 250 000 people found  that ala was linked to a 14 lower risk  of heart attacks  numerous studies have also linked ala to  a lower risk of stroke so if you're a  vegetarian or you don't eat fish flax  seeds can be your best source of omega-3  fats and ala  it will not only boost your heart health  but will also prevent chronic damage to  your blood vessels from toxins  prevent stroke and tumor growth  number three flax seeds are a rich  source of lignans which may reduce  cancer risk  lignins are plant compounds that have  antioxidant and estrogenic properties  both of which can help lower the risk of  cancer and improve health  interestingly flax seeds are one of the  richest sources of lignans and contains  up to 800 times more lignans than other  plant foods  observational studies show that those  who eat flax seeds have a lower risk of  breast cancer particularly in females  beyond 50 or those who are  post-menopausal additionally according  to a canadian study involving more than  6 000 females those who ate flax seeds  were 18 percent less likely to develop  breast cancer  men can also benefit from eating flax  seeds in a small study men who were  given 30 grams of flax seeds a day while  following a low fat diet showed reduced  levels of a prostate cancer marker  suggesting a lower risk of prostate  cancer  flax seeds have appeared to have the  potential to prevent colon and skin  cancer in labs and animal studies yet  more research is needed to confirm this  nevertheless the evidence thus far  points to flax seeds being a potentially  valuable food in the fight against  various cancers  number four flax seeds are rich in  dietary fiber  just one tablespoon of flax seeds  contains three grams of fiber which is  eight to 12 percent of the daily  recommended intake  flax seeds contain two types of dietary  fiber soluble dietary fiber and  insoluble dietary fiber the fiber gets  fermented by the bacteria in the large  bowl this bulks up the stools and  results in more regular bowel movements  on one hand the soluble fiber increases  the consistency of the contents of your  intestine and slows down the digestion  rate  this has been shown to definitely help  in regulating blood sugar levels and  decreasing cholesterol levels  similarly on the other hand the  insoluble fiber allows more water to  bind to the stools which increases their  bulk and results in softer stools this  is especially useful for preventing  constipation and relieves symptoms for  those who suffer from irritable bowel  syndrome or diverticular disease  number fifth health benefit of flax  seeds is that they help to lower blood  cholesterol levels  in one study people with high blood  cholesterol levels were selected and  half of the people were given 30 grams  of flaxseed powder daily for 3 months  when compared to the ones who didn't  consume flax seed powder the total  cholesterol levels lowered by 17 percent  and bad ldl cholesterol levels lowered  by almost 20 percent  in another study of people with diabetes  researchers found that taking 10 grams  of flaxseed powder daily for one month  resulted in a 12 increase in good hdl  cholesterol levels  these effects appear to be due to the  fiber present in flax seeds as it binds  to the bile salts and is then excreted  by the body to replenish these biosols  cholesterol is pulled from your blood  into your liver this process eventually  lowers your blood cholesterol levels  number six flax seeds may help to lower  blood pressure levels  studies on flax seeds have also focused  on its natural ability to lower blood  pressure a canadian study found that  eating 30 grams of flax seeds daily for  six months lowered systolic and  diastolic blood pressure by 10  millimeters and 7 millimeters of mercury  respectively for those who are already  taking blood pressure medication flax  seeds lowered blood pressure even  further it was also seen that regular  flax seed consumption decreased the  number of patients with uncontrolled  high blood pressure levels by more than  17 percent  furthermore according to the large  review that looked at data from 11  studies revealed that taking flax seeds  daily for more than 3 months lowered  blood pressure levels by 2 millimeters  mercury  you may feel that this 2 millimeter  mercury fall is insignificant but a 2  millimeter mercury reduction in blood  pressure can lower the risk of dying  from stroke by 10  and from heart disease by more than 7  percent  number 7 flax seeds contain high quality  protein  flax seeds are a great source of  plant-based protein and there is growing  interest in flaxseed protein and its  health benefits flaxseed protein is rich  in amino acids such as arginine aspartic  acid and glutamic acid numerous lab and  animal studies have shown that flaxseed  protein helped to improve immune  function lowered cholesterol prevented  tumors and had anti-fungal properties  also  if you are considering cutting back on  meat and worried that you will go hungry  flax seeds may be just your answer in  fact in one study 21 adults were given  an animal protein or plant protein meal  the study found no difference in terms  of appetite satiety or food intake noted  between the two meals the likely  explanation is that both the animal and  plant protein meals stimulate hormones  in the gut to bring about the feeling of  fullness which eventually results in  eating less also known as early satiety  number eight flax seeds can also help to  control blood sugar levels  type 2 diabetes is a major health  problem worldwide millions of people are  suffering from uncontrolled sugar levels  and eventual complications that develop  type 2 diabetes is characterized by high  blood sugar levels as a result of either  the bodies inability to secrete insulin  or the body becoming resistant to it  a few studies have found that people  with type 2 diabetes who added 10 to 20  grams of flaxseed powder to their daily  diet for at least one month saw  reductions of 8 to 20 percent in their  blood sugar levels  this blood sugar lowering effect is  notably due to the flax seeds insoluble  fiber content  researchers have found that insoluble  fiber slows down the release of sugar  into the blood and reduces blood sugar  levels  however one study found no change in  blood sugar levels or any improvement in  diabetes management  even though more research is still  needed and results are confusing flax  seeds can be a beneficial and nutritious  addition to the diet of people with  diabetes  if you'd like to know more about the  best diet for diabetes click the banner  above to watch our video i will also  provide a link in the description box  down below  number nine health benefit of flax seed  is that they can decrease hunger which  can aid in weight control  now if you have the tendency to snack  between meals you should consider trying  flax seeds as they are great for  munching and are very effective in  reducing hunger pangs  one study found that adding 2.5 grams of  flax fiber extract to a beverage reduce  the feelings of hunger and overall  appetite  the feelings of reduced hunger were  likely due to the soluble fiber content  of flax seeds  the fiber in flax seeds slows digestion  in the stomach which triggers a host of  hormones that control appetite and  provide a feeling of fullness  flax seeds dietary fiber can help in  weight control by suppressing hunger and  increasing the feelings of fullness  this is especially useful in diabetic  and hypertensive patients who are  actively trying to lose weight by  controlling their appetite naturally  number 10 flax seeds are a versatile  ingredient  flax seeds or flaxseed oil can be added  to many common foods you can try the  following methods to make the most out  of flax seeds adding them to water and  drinking it as a part of your daily  fluid intake  drizzling flaxseed oil as a dressing on  salad  sprinkling grounded flax seeds over your  breakfast cereal  mixing them into your favorite yogurt  adding them into cookies muffins breads  or other batters  mixing them into smoothies to thicken up  the consistency  and incorporating them into meat patties  remember to always opt for grounded flax  seeds as they are easier to digest whole  flax seeds are not useful because your  intestines cannot break down the tough  outer shell of the seeds that being said  you can still buy whole flax seeds grind  them in a coffee grinder and store the  grounded flax seeds in an airtight  container  with many proven health benefits and  possibly more there is no better time  than now to grab some flax seeds from  your local grocery store or you can also  buy them online by clicking the links in  the description box down below  thank you so much for watching this  video and i hope you liked it to support  us become a member of our mid-simplified  youtube channel or support us via  patreon do comment below about any  feedback you would like us to know so  friends take care and see you in our  next video
 

